AB  - Backend system outages frequently originate from deployments involving code changes, dependency upgrades, configuration modifications, or database migrations. While existing AIOps approaches primarily focus on post-incident detection and root cause analysis, this paper proposes CanaryRisk, a framework that shifts risk assessment to the early-canary stage of the release pipeline. CanaryRisk jointly models code diff features, configuration diff features, historical release records, and early-stage canary telemetry to produce an interpretable risk score that guides rollout decisions: continue, hold, or rollback. We employ a gradient boosting model with SHAP-based explainability, favoring transparency over complex deep learning. We evaluate CanaryRisk on Google's Online Boutique microservice demo by simulating five fault categories. Results show an AUC of 0.946, a 2.9-minute reduction in mean time to warning versus the single-metric threshold baseline, a false positive rate of 5.8%, 88.3% rollback recommendation accuracy, and 91.7% explanation accuracy in identifying the dominant fault factor.
ER  - 

