AB  - The inherent complexity, scale, and dynamic nature of cloud computing environments render them profoundly prone to a spectrum of anomalies, including performance degradation, security breaches, and resource failures. These incidents directly threaten Service Level Agreements (SLAs), compromise security postures, and lead to operational cost overruns. To address these challenges, Machine Learning (ML) has emerged as a critical tool for proactive and intelligent anomaly detection. This survey provides a systematic review and taxonomy of state-of-the-art ML techniques developed for this purpose. This work categorizes the existing literature along three key dimensions: (i) the anomaly type (point, contextual, collective), (ii) the underlying ML approach (supervised, unsupervised, semi-supervised, and deep learning), and (iii) the target cloud resource (e.g., VM, container, network, application). The review synthesizes key findings on the strengths and limitations of each paradigm, identifies the most prevalent data sources—namely metrics, logs, and traces—and discusses common evaluation benchmarks. Our analysis concludes that while deep learning models demonstrate significant promise in handling complex, high-dimensional cloud data, critical challenges persist concerning data quality, model explainability, and real-time adaptation in evolving environments. Finally, we outline promising future research directions, focusing on the integration with AIOps platforms, the application of Explainable AI (XAI) for root cause analysis, and the adoption of federated learning for privacy-preserving, collaborative detection in multi-tenant clouds.
ER  - 

