AB  - Cloud computing systems generate big amounts of operational logs with useful information on the performance, security breaches, and service behaviours. Nevertheless, these logs cannot be effectively monitored manually and cannot identify sophisticated anomalies instantly. In order to overcome this dilemma, this paper suggests a Machine Learning-Based Cloud Log Anomaly Detection and Resolution System, which combines statistical anomaly detection, time-series failure prediction, severity classification, and rule-based automated resolution, into a cloud-native monitoring system. The system takes in and preprocesses massive cloud logs, identifies structured operational characteristics and uses statistical outlier detection to determine abnormal trends in system events. Prediction of the possible failures in service delivery is then done using temporal trend analysis to prevent failure before it takes place. Moreover, a log severity classification module ranks critical incidences and a rule-based expert resolution engine suggests remedial measures like restarting services, checking configurations or scaling. The suggested architecture is deployed to scalable cloud services to make the log ingestion and processing efficient. It is experimentally shown that the system has an anomaly detection accuracy of 97.1%, prediction accuracy of 96.8% and a success rate of automated resolution of 98%. The given results show that the suggested intelligent monitoring system can contribute to the cloud-level reliability to a significant extent, minimize the efforts that have to be put in the work of the manual operations and make the system management proactive and driven by AIOps in the recent cloud infrastructures.
ER  - 

