AB  - The cloud infrastructures deployed in the modern world have mission-critical services that need quick recovery of faults and continuous availability. The traditional automation systems cannot handle dynamic workloads and variable failure modes and frequently experience delayed recovery and service degradation. The existing self-healing strategies are mostly based on fixed rules or individual learning modules. This restricts their capability to adapt policies and justify recovery actions in real time. To address these limitations, this study proposes a self-healing cloud infrastructure observability-driven agentic AI model. The proposed framework incorporates the observability, agentic reasoning and reinforcement learning based policy adaptation in a closed-loop control architecture that integrates fault detection, decision making, and remediation alongside the continuous verification of state. Experimental evaluation under controlled service fault injections shows a 25% reduction in mean recovery time compared with reinforcement-learning-only recovery models and nearly 30% improvement over playbook-based automation. The system also achieves 98.3% service availability, outperforming agentic AIOps and Kubernetes-centric healing baselines. These findings prove that agent-controlled autonomous control can enhance cloud resilience by over 2 % in the case of sustained faults and boost the development of fully autonomous management of cloud infrastructure.
ER  - 

