AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) show promise for Root Cause Analysis (RCA) in cloud-native systems but suffer from hallucinations, context constraints, and dynamic dependency misunderstandings. This paper presents TAMO-FoA (ToolAugmented Multimodal Operations Framework for AIOps), a comprehensive framework addressing these limitations through four key innovations: tool-augmented perception, SOP-guided reasoning, specialized hallucination detection, and multi-modal fusion. Our framework integrates a hybrid multi-modal encoder combining 1D-CNN, BERT+LSTM, and GNN architectures with diffusion-based fusion for temporal causality preservation across heterogeneous telemetry data. The SOP-enhanced ReAct agent incorporates Standard Operating Procedures to guide diagnostic workflows and reduce irrelevant tool calls by 42%. Our novel HDM-2 hallucination detector employs dual verification mechanisms achieving 21% relative improvement in F1 scores over existing approaches. Experimental evaluation on real-world microservice failures demonstrates 17.2% accuracy improvement and $2.3 \times$ token efficiency gains compared to baseline methods. A 3-month production deployment achieved 76.3% root cause identification accuracy with 34% reduction in mean time to resolution. TAMO-FoA provides a concrete path toward dependable AgentOps for self-healing cloud infrastructures, with complete implementation publicly available at https://github.com/akshaymittal143/TAMO-FoA.
