AB  - As aviation, aerospace, maritime, and ground transportation systems evolve toward higher levels of intelligence and autonomy, their operational states become increasingly complex and their information sources more diverse, leading to a substantial rise in the cognitive burden placed on human operators during monitoring, comprehension, and decision-making. Against this backdrop, achieving a consistent operational logic across heterogeneous vehicles and mission contexts has become a critical requirement for future human - autonomy teaming. Prognostics and Health Management (PHM) provides the foundational capabilities for simplified operation; however, existing PHM implementations often suffer from fragmented information flows, non-standardized model interfaces, and opaque autonomous reasoning, which collectively hinder high-transparency and high-trust collaboration. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a unified PHM operation interface framework applicable to aviation, aerospace, maritime, and ground vehicles, built upon the OSA-CBM seven-layer architecture and the autonomy criteria of AIOps. The framework integrates state monitoring, degradation-mechanism modeling, fault diagnosis, multi-model fusion - based Remaining Useful Life (RUL) prediction, and cost-sensitive decision support within a single structural pipeline, and presents these functions through interpretable and transparent visualizations to facilitate effective human autonomy collaboration. To validate the framework, a degradation dataset is constructed using a coupled-increment degradation model for aero-engine components. Softmax-based fault diagnosis, HSMM-based temporal modeling, similarity-based trajectory matching, $\varepsilon$-SVR regression, and information-entropy fusion are combined to form an end-to-end example spanning degradation simulation to autonomous recommendation. Results show that the unified interface significantly enhances situational-awareness consistency, reduces operator workload, and improves safety and interpretability in complex scenarios through joint presentation of degradation trends, probabilistic inference, and risk-cost structures. The proposed framework demonstrates a generalizable framework with potential cross-domain applicability and provides a viable pathway toward unified operational logic in next-generation intelligent transportation systems.
ER  - 

