AB  - The cloud-native, service-based architecture of the 5G Core (5GC) produces high-volume multivariate telemetry with strong cross-metric dependencies, heterogeneous metric semantics, and non-stationary behavior caused by traffic cycles and software upgrades. These properties often destabilize unsupervised anomaly detection and increase false alarms in operational AIOps pipelines. This paper presents SCT-5GC, an engineering-oriented self-conditioned two-pass Transformer reconstructor for anomaly detection in 5GC telemetry. In the first pass, the model reconstructs normal behavior and computes an element-wise residual map. In the second pass, this residual structure is concatenated with the original input and processed through pass-specific projections to guide a refined reconstruction. This residual-conditioned refinement improves sensitivity to consistent cross-metric deviations while suppressing benign fluctuations. To support deployment-oriented alerting, SCT-5GC calibrates the decision threshold on benign validation windows using quantile calibration, enabling controllable nominal false-alarm rates without test-label leakage. Experiments on operational 5GC telemetry from the ENCQOR testbed (Encqor5G) show that SCT-5GC achieves a precision of 0.891, recall of 0.745, and F1-score of 0.811, outperforming the strongest baseline, TranAD (F1 = 0.738), while also attaining an AUROC of 0.90 and a PR-AUC of 0.81. Additional experiments on public industrial datasets (SWaT and WADI) provide contextual evidence that the refinement mechanism remains effective beyond telecom telemetry.
