AB  - In modern infrastructure dominated by cloudnative architectures and microservices, massive heterogeneous logs have become core assets for Artificial Intelligence for IT Operations (AIOps) and security analysis. However, traditional data mining methods are limited by their “correlation, not causation” nature. When dealing with Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs) and non-linear cascading failures, these methods face challenges such as high false alarm rates, lack of interpretability, and difficulty in supporting automated intervention. This paper systematically reconstructs the technical framework of log-based causal analysis for security and operations, advocating for a paradigm shift from passive “correlation discovery” to active “causal intervention”. We analyze four cutting-edge technical routes: explicit dependency mining based on provenance graphs, implicit structure discovery based on statistics, causal representation based on deep learning, and Large Language Model (LLM)-driven semantic fusion and agent architectures. This paper reveals the cognitive disputes, theoretical challenges, and technical difficulties existing models face when moving toward rigorous causal inference. Finally, we propose the future visions of “Causal-Native Logging” and “Neuro-symbolic Agents”.
