AB  - The rapid digitalization of smart grid infrastructure has driven an explosive growth in the scale and complexity of distributed monitoring systems. In modern power grid operations, real-time and reliable collection of massive telemetry metrics—spanning generation, transmission, substation, and distribution layers—is mission-critical for ensuring grid stability, fault detection, and intelligent dispatch. However, large-scale cloud-native monitoring architectures based on Prometheus and Kafka face critical challenges in maintaining load balance and low collection latency, particularly during sudden traffic bursts triggered by grid fault events or peak-load fluctuations. Traditional reactive scheduling strategies, such as Round-Robin, suffer from fundamental allocation “blindness,” remaining entirely oblivious to the heterogeneous resource demands of collection tasks and the dynamic load states of monitoring nodes. This inevitably leads to localized resource bottlenecks, collection queue blockages, and severe metric data drop rates—directly compromising the timeliness of grid fault alerts and the integrity of power system observability. To address these critical challenges in smart grid AIOps infrastructure, this paper proposes a novel Predictive Greedy Scheduling (PGS) strategy based on Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCN) and multi-source metric feature fusion. First, a comprehensive multi-source metric feature extraction mechanism is designed to capture the complex temporal dependencies of node workloads by fusing heterogeneous indicators including CPU utilization, memory footprint, network I/O throughput, and scrape queue length. Subsequently, the TCN model—leveraging dilated causal convolutions and residual connections—accurately forecasts impending load trends with minimal latency, enabling a greedy heuristic algorithm to preemptively route data collection tasks to nodes with ample anticipated capacity. Extensive experiments conducted on a simulated Prometheus and Kafka cluster demonstrate the superiority of the proposed approach. The TCN model achieves an ultra-low inference latency of $\mathbf{1 2. 4} \mathbf{ m s}$ and a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 0.045, significantly outperforming baseline LSTM and ARIMA models. Furthermore, end-to-end system evaluations reveal that the PGS strategy reduces the P99 collection latency by 18% and improves global load balancing by 22% compared to the traditional Round-Robin method, while reducing the metric data point drop rate from $\mathbf{1. 5 \%}$ to below $\mathbf{0. 2 \%}$.
ER  - 

