AB  - The recent developments in the field of Artificial Intelligence (AI), especially with respect to cloudhosted Large Language Models (LLM), lead to a rising demand for electrical energy. AI compute centres are becoming major electricity consumers and their integration into energy systems causes challenges and provide new opportunities. At the same time, fluctuating electricity production from wind and solar power becomes abundant and cheap. Such generation even overloads the public grid capacity. To optimize the fit between production (regional wind and solar parks) and consumption (AI compute centres) is therefore an urgent task where engineering methodology is lacking. The optimal integration of AI computing load into energy grids from the perspective of the effective use of fluctuating renewable energies, grid-friendly regulation of computing load, and the development of regional energy and computing systems is a key to that problem. For this, AI models need to be adapted with regard to distributability and computing load orchestration, and sector-specific AI models for dynamic optimization need to be developed and trained, same as AI-based frame-works (AIOps) for energy-aware and optimized data centre operation. This contribution provides a mathematical model for such an integrated energy and compute system which can be used for dimensioning, and optimization in design and operation. The model is developed based on case studies from Germany. A discussion of further research concludes the contribution and provides an outlook.
