AB  - Cloud-scale microservices make it easy for failures to spread across RPCs, queues, and data stores because of closely coupled, rapidly changing dependencies. Metrics, logs, and traces can only see part of the picture. Traditional "self-healing" is generally reactive and compartmentalized, with static thresholds, pointwise autoscaling, or single-stage AIOps. Telemetry (OpenTelemetry metrics/logs/traces) sends data to a hybrid anomaly ensemble made up of STL-Z, one-class learners, Transformer forecasters, and BOCPD/RuLSIF. SWARM-HEAL saves $5.4k and 12.7 kWh for every 1 million requests, with a rollback rate of 2.1%. These results indicate that combining causal-twin RCA with context-aware hybrid swarms and hierarchical MARL leads to faster, safer, and cheaper self-healing. This improves the reliability of autonomous clouds when they are under real workloads and incident mixes.
ER  - 

