AB  - With the rapid development of cloud computing and microservice architectures, IT Operation and Maintenance (O&M) systems generate massive amounts of monitoring data. Traditional O&M dashboards often suffer from information overload and visual clutter, leading to a “data-rich, information-poor” dilemma that severely hinders rapid decision-making during system anomalies. To address this issue, this paper proposes a novel dashboard visual optimization strategy driven by a two-stage indicator screening method. First, a Random Forest (RF) algorithm is employed to evaluate the global importance of 28 multi-dimensional raw O&M metrics, filtering out long-tail indicators. Subsequently, a Spearman rank correlation analysis is utilized to eliminate highly collinear redundant metrics, successfully reducing the feature space to 8 core decision indicators. Furthermore, we introduce an innovative mapping mechanism that translates the algorithmic importance scores into normalized visual weights for dashboard layout design. Experimental results on a real-world smart government cloud dataset demonstrate that our method achieves a 71.4% reduction in dimensionality with a negligible classification accuracy loss of 0.4%. Crucially, A/B testing in simulated emergency scenarios reveals that dashboards designed with our weight-layout strategy reduce the average fault identification and response time by 23.2% and decrease the subjective cognitive load of O&M personnel by 45.1%. This research bridges the gap between machine learning-based feature selection and human-computer interaction design, providing a highly efficient and scalable paradigm for modern AIOps visualization.
ER  - 

